    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Offer Breakdown & Analysis\n\n#### Comparing the Numbers\n| Component | Meta (IC6 PM) | Google (L6 PM Offer) | Notes / Variance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **-$5,000** (Google's band goes up to $271k) |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **+$11,000** (Google target is slightly higher) |\n| **Annualized Equity (Avg)**| ~$250,000/yr | $270,000/yr ($1.08M / 4) | **+$20,000/yr** (4-year straight average) |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | Missing |\n| **Annualized TC (Avg)** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** | **+~5% annualized increase** |\n\n---\n\n### 2. How Google\u2019s Vesting Schedule Impacts This Offer\nGoogle\u2019s initial equity grants follow a **front-loaded vesting schedule** (typically 33% in Year 1, 33% in Year 2, 22% in Year 3, and 12% in Year 4):\n\n* **Year 1 TC:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $356.4k stock (33%) = **~$638,400**\n* **Year 2 TC:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $356.4k stock (33%) = **~$638,400**\n* **Year 3 TC:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $237.6k stock (22%) + refreshes = **~$520k + refreshes**\n* **Year 4 TC:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $129.6k stock (12%) + refreshes = **~$412k + refreshes**\n\n**Takeaway:** Years 1 and 2 deliver higher liquid cash flow than your current Meta rate ($638k vs. $526k). However, on an annualized steady-state basis (~$552k), it is only a **~5% total comp bump** and includes a **base salary reduction** from what you make today.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Verdict: Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n**Do not accept as-is. You should negotiate.**\n\n* **You hold maximum leverage:** You were reached out to passively, you are performing well and happy at Meta, and staying is a completely viable outcome.\n* **The offer has room:** An annualized premium of ~5% is not enough to justify switching companies, restarting performance history, and rebuilding political capital.\n* **Google's offer has clear gaps:** No sign-on bonus, a lower base salary than your current level, and an equity grant sitting around the 50th percentile for Bay Area L6 PMs.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Exactly to Ask For\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Counter-Offer Target | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | You make $240k at Meta; taking a base cut is unnecessary when Google\u2019s posted band goes to $271k. |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Bridges unvested equity timing and unvested performance bonus cycles at Meta. |\n| **Equity (4-Yr GSU)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,350,000** | L6 top-band equity grants typically range from $1.2M to $1.4M+. |\n\n**Total Revised Target (Year 1):** ~$740k\u2013$800k (including sign-on)  \n**Total Revised Target (Annualized Avg):** ~$615k\u2013$645k/yr\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Strategy & Script\n\nDo not provide exact breakdown demands immediately; instead, anchor on your current situation, the switching friction, and the overall target that would make this an easy \"yes.\"\n\n#### Talking Points for the Recruiter:\n1. **Express enthusiasm:** Reiterate genuine interest in the team and product scope.\n2. **Anchor on Meta's baseline:** Mention that you are well-compensated and happy at Meta IC6, making ~$526k+, with a higher base salary ($240k) and strong performance momentum.\n3. **Point out the gap:** Explain that moving to a new organization carries switching risk, and an offer with a lower base salary and no sign-on bonus doesn't create enough of a financial incentive to leave.\n\n#### Sample Script / Email:\n\n> *\"Thanks again for putting this offer together. I\u2019ve enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m genuinely excited about the scope of the [Team/Product] role.*\n>\n> *I\u2019ve reviewed the numbers in detail. Because I wasn't actively looking and am in a great spot at Meta\u2014currently at $240k base with ~$526k total annual compensation\u2014this package is currently near parity with my current trajectory once you factor in the base salary reduction and the friction of leaving unvested equity/bonus cycles behind.*\n> \n> *I\u2019d love to make this move work. If we can get the base closer to **$255k\u2013$260k**, increase the initial equity grant to **$1.3M**, and include a **$75k\u2013$100k sign-on bonus**, this would become a clear decision for me and I would be ready to sign.\"*",
